# drirc delivery smoke: how the conservative-LRZ arm is delivered (no game, no build)
# date: 2026-11-22T22:14:47+08:00
# driver that answers: the system Turnip (/usr/lib64/libvulkan_freedreno.so), same Mesa 27 base as
#   the ship stack build/out sha256 4c3cdb210082
# command in every section: TU_DEBUG=startup vulkaninfo --summary   (grep 'TU:')
# mechanism under test: Mesa reads a drirc directory from $DRIRC_CONFIGDIR, and setting it
#   REPLACES $datadir/drirc.d (src/util/xmlconfig.c:1543-1552), which is why the distro file is
#   copied into the directory next to the experiment file.
# limit of this proof: it shows a device-level <option> is parsed and applied. It does not name
#   tu_disable_conservative_lrz, which has no log line; for that option the same file, the same
#   section and the same name spelling as the generator declares is all the evidence there is.

## A - default config dir (no DRIRC_CONFIGDIR): expect the Mesa default BANDWIDTH
TU: info: TU_DEBUG=0x1
TU: info: Created an instance
TU: info: Found compatible device '/dev/dri/renderD128' (msm).
TU: info: TU_AUTOTUNE: Algorithm: BANDWIDTH, Mod Flags: 0x0 (), Metric Flags: 0x2 (SAMPLES )

## B - DRIRC_CONFIGDIR with a file that sets tu_autotune_algorithm=prefer_sysmem at device level
directory contents:
00-turnip-defaults.conf
90-experiment.conf
TU: info: TU_DEBUG=0x1
TU: info: Created an instance
TU: info: Found compatible device '/dev/dri/renderD128' (msm).
TU: info: TU_AUTOTUNE_ALGO=3 (prefer_sysmem)
TU: info: TU_AUTOTUNE: Algorithm: PREFER_SYSMEM, Mod Flags: 0x0 (), Metric Flags: 0x0 ()

## C - the file the game arm uses: the LRZ option only, in the same device-level form
    <device driver="turnip">
        <option name="tu_disable_conservative_lrz" value="true" />
    </device>
TU: info: TU_DEBUG=0x1
TU: info: Created an instance
TU: info: Found compatible device '/dev/dri/renderD128' (msm).
TU: info: TU_AUTOTUNE: Algorithm: BANDWIDTH, Mod Flags: 0x0 (), Metric Flags: 0x2 (SAMPLES )
(BANDWIDTH again: this file sets no autotune option.  It loads without an error line, but see D:
 below - an unknown option name is silent too, so "no error line" is not proof of recognition.)

## D - control: the same directory with a deliberately unknown option name
unknown knob, expect a Mesa error line naming it
TU: info: TU_DEBUG=0x1
TU: info: Created an instance
TU: info: Found compatible device '/dev/dri/renderD128' (msm).
TU: info: TU_AUTOTUNE: Algorithm: BANDWIDTH, Mod Flags: 0x0 (), Metric Flags: 0x2 (SAMPLES )

## What this does and does not prove
- Proved: a device-level <option> in a $DRIRC_CONFIGDIR directory reaches Turnip (B against A).
- Proved: the experiment file is the same shape and loads without an error line (C).
- NOT proved: that tu_disable_conservative_lrz specifically took effect. D shows this Mesa build
  ignores an unrecognised option name in silence, so a misspelling and an applied option look
  identical in a log. The only evidence for the name is that it is copied from
  src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_drirc_gen.py:48 (declared name and c_name are identical), and the
  measurement itself is the check: if the LRZ counters do not move at all between the arms,
  the honest reading is 'no effect or not delivered', and the tie-breaker is a built driver
  with the flag flipped in code, not another log line.
